---
import ToolPage from '../../components/tools/ToolPage.astro';
import { fireNumber, sustainableRate, yearsToTarget } from '../../tools/closed-form';
import { fireYears, historyTest } from '../../tools/engine/fire';
import { HISTORY_DATASET } from '../../tools/engine/model';
import { money, pct, wholeYears } from '../../tools/format';
import { MARKET_DATASETS } from '@glideworth/core';

const m = (x: number) => money(x, 'USD');
const ds = MARKET_DATASETS[HISTORY_DATASET];
// Worked example, computed by the calculator's own code at build time.
const ex = { age: 30, spend: 40_000, wr: 4, balance: 50_000, save: 20_000, ret: 5, tax: 0, hy: 30, stocks: 60, timing: 'end', cur: 'USD' } as const;
const target = fireNumber(ex.spend, ex.wr / 100);
const engine = fireYears(ex);
const formula = yearsToTarget(ex.balance, ex.save, 0.05, target, 'end');
const hist = historyTest({ balance: target, spend: ex.spend, years: 30, stockShare: 0.6, taxRate: 0, timing: 'start', age: 60, cur: 'USD' });
const rows = [3, 3.5, 4, 4.5, 5].map((r) => [`${r}%`, `${(100 / r).toFixed(1)}×`, m(fireNumber(ex.spend, r / 100))]);

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is a FIRE number?',
    a: `<p>The portfolio you would need to stop working: yearly spending divided by a withdrawal rate. At 4% that is 25 times spending, so ${m(40_000)} a year gives ${m(target)}.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Is 4% a safe withdrawal rate?',
    a: '<p>It is a rule of thumb from studies of US history over roughly 30-year retirements, with fixed real spending and, in the main cases, no costs or tax. It was never a guarantee. Longer retirements, other markets, fees and tax all push the rate down in some calculations; flexible spending pushes it up.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does tax change my FIRE number?',
    a: '<p>If withdrawals are taxed, you have to withdraw more than you spend. The calculator’s flat tax option grosses the number up: at a flat 15%, 40,000 to spend needs about 47,059 withdrawn, so the 25× number becomes about 1,176,471. It is a single rate you choose, not a country tax estimate.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a separate FIRE number calculator?',
    a: '<p>This page is both: it gives the FIRE number and the years of saving to reach it. If you only want the number, read it from the first result.</p>',
  },
];
---

<ToolPage
  id="fire"
  heading="FIRE calculator: your FIRE number and when you reach it"
  lede="Your FIRE number from your spending and a withdrawal rate, the years of saving it takes, and how that number would have fared in US market history. It runs the Glideworth planning engine in your browser; nothing you type is sent anywhere."
  faq={faq}
  adds="A FIRE number is a multiple of one spending figure."
  related={[
    { href: '/blog/coast-fire-explained/', label: 'Coast FIRE explained' },
    { href: '/features/solvers/', label: 'Solvers: required portfolio and safe withdrawal rate for your own plan' },
    { href: '/docs/methodology/historical-data/', label: 'The historical data the engine uses' },
    { href: '/compare/spreadsheets/', label: 'Glideworth, spreadsheets and free calculators' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>How the calculator works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>FIRE number</strong> = yearly spending ÷ withdrawal rate. With the optional flat tax on withdrawals, the
      spending is first grossed up: spending ÷ (1 − tax rate).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Years to reach it.</strong> The engine projects your invested balance with your yearly saving added each
      year at your return after inflation, and marks financial independence at the first year end where the portfolio
      is at or above the FIRE number.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A test of the number.</strong> The engine then starts a portfolio of exactly your FIRE number in every
      year of US history from {ds.firstYear} that leaves room for the retirement length you chose (30 years by
      default), spends your spending each year rising with inflation, and counts the start years in which every year was
      paid. The mix is 60% stocks and 40% bonds by default, rebalanced yearly.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The formulas</h2>
  <p class="formula">FIRE number = spending ÷ withdrawal rate</p>
  <p class="formula">years = ln((T × r + C′) ÷ (B × r + C′)) ÷ ln(1 + r)</p>
  <p>
    <code>T</code> is the FIRE number, <code>B</code> what you have invested, <code>r</code> the return after
    inflation and <code>C′</code> the yearly saving moved to the year end under the timing you chose (C × (1 + r) for
    the start of the year, C × (1 + r)<sup>½</sup> for the middle, C for the end).
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="Table, scrolls sideways if it does not fit">
  <table>
    <caption>FIRE number for spending of {m(40_000)}</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>Withdrawal rate</th><th>Multiple of spending</th><th>FIRE number</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{rows.map((r) => <tr>{r.map((c) => <td>{c}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    Computed by the calculator’s own code when this page was built. Spending of {m(40_000)} at 4% gives
    {' '}{m(target)}. A 30-year-old with {m(50_000)} invested saves {m(20_000)} at the end of each year at 5% after
    inflation. The formula gives {formula.toFixed(2)} years; the engine, checking each year end, first finds the
    portfolio at or above {m(target)} after {engine.years === null ? 'more than its horizon' : wholeYears(engine.years)},
    when it holds {m(engine.path[engine.years ?? 0] ?? 0)}.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tested against history ({ds.label}), a portfolio of {m(target)} paying {m(40_000)} a year in today’s money, 60%
    stocks, withdrawals at the start of each year, paid every year of a 30-year retirement in
    {' '}{Math.round(hist.successRate * hist.trials)} of {hist.trials} start years from {hist.firstYear} to
    {' '}{hist.lastStartYear} ({pct(hist.successRate, 1)}).{
      hist.failures.length > 0 &&
        ` The shortest-lived started in ${hist.failures[0]!.startYear} and paid ${wholeYears(hist.failures[0]!.yearsFunded)} in full.`
    }
  </p>

  <h2>Where 4% came from, and its limits</h2>
  <p>
    William Bengen’s 1994 article <a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.financialplanningassociation.org/sites/default/files/2021-04/MAR04%20Determining%20Withdrawal%20Rates%20Using%20Historical%20Data.pdf"
      >“Determining Withdrawal Rates Using Historical Data”</a
    > (<em>Journal of Financial Planning</em>) and the 1998 Trinity study by Cooley, Hubbard and Walz (<a rel="noopener noreferrer"
      href="https://www.aaii.com/journal/article/retirement-savings-choosing-a-withdrawal-rate-that-is-sustainable"
      >AAII Journal</a
    >) tested withdrawals against US history from 1926 over retirements of about 30 years, with spending fixed in real
    terms and, in the main cases, no tax or costs. A 4% first-year withdrawal survived the start years they tested. Each
    of those conditions is a limit: a 40- or 50-year retirement is a different problem, other countries’ history was
    harsher (Wade Pfau’s <a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://financialplanningassociation.org/article/journal/DEC10-international-perspective-safe-withdrawal-rates-demise-4-percent-rule"
      >international study</a
    > found 4% “safe” in only 4 of 17 countries), and fees and tax come straight off the return.
  </p>
  <p>
    With a steady return, the rate that spends a portfolio exactly over <code>n</code> years is r ÷ (1 − (1 + r)<sup
      >−n</sup
    >): {pct(sustainableRate(0.03, 30), 2)} for 30 years at 3% after inflation, but {pct(sustainableRate(0.03, 50), 2)}{' '}
    for 50 years. That is an upper bound, because it has no volatility in it.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the engine and the formula can differ</h2>
  <p>
    The FIRE number is a definition, so it is the same in both. For the years to reach it, the formula gives the exact
    fractional point; the engine reports the first whole year at or above the number, so it is the formula rounded up.
    Both credit each year’s saving with the share of the year’s return set by the timing option (the engine’s default
    is the middle of the year).
  </p>

  <h2>High spending and a longer retirement</h2>
  <p>
    This page is the general case: the spending you enter, a 4% starting withdrawal rate and a 30-year history test.
    High yearly spending, and a retirement that may run much longer than 30 years, are on the <a
      href="/tools/fat-fire-calculator/">fat FIRE</a
    > calculator. It starts from a higher spending level and a 3.5% withdrawal rate, and it compares that target with
    lean and regular spending for the same person.
  </p>

  <h2>What this assumes and leaves out</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>One steady return while you save, the same every year; real returns vary.</li>
    <li>Spending fixed in today’s money for the whole retirement, with no pension or other income to lower it.</li>
    <li>No tax unless you set the flat rate, which is a single rate you choose, not a country estimate.</li>
    <li>
      The history test uses one country’s history ({ds.label}); its result is what happened there, not a probability.
      Data: Aswath Damodaran, <em>Historical Returns on Stocks, Bonds and Bills</em> (NYU Stern), and US Bureau of Labor
      Statistics CPI-U.
    </li>
    <li>No fees, no contribution limits and no accounts locked until an access age.</li>
  </ul>
</ToolPage>
